course: dictionary
lesson: 15926
title: Two meanings of one word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word unqualified is one of them. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer means. The clues are in the sentence.
explain: «The builder was unqualified, so he had no certificate to show us.» Here the clue is certificate. Unqualified means you do not have the training or papers needed for a job.
explain: «The team had an unqualified success and won every game.» Here the clue is success and won every game. Unqualified means complete, with no limits. Ask yourself: which meaning fits the sentence?
fact: Unqualified means not having the training, skills or certificate needed for a job.
fact: Unqualified also means complete, without limits, as in «an unqualified success».
fact: «Unqualified to» means you are not trained or allowed to do something, as in «unqualified to teach».
fact: An unqualified electrician may be dangerous, because they may not know how to do the work safely.
fact: To check a meaning, swap it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The job needs a certificate, so an unqualified person cannot do it.» What does unqualified mean here? | Very tired after a long day | *Without the training or certificate needed | Completely finished with the work | Wearing a uniform
exercise: «Our team won every game. It was an unqualified success.» What does «an unqualified success» mean? | *A complete success, with no limits | A small success for only one player | A success that happened by accident | A failure that nobody noticed
exercise: Why might an unqualified electrician be dangerous? | They are too tall to climb ladders | They work only on Sundays | They always wear gloves and hats | *They may not have the training to do the work safely
test: «She was unqualified to drive the bus.» What does unqualified to mean here? | Too late for it | Happy about it | *Not trained or allowed to do it | Very good at it
test: Sam says, «The show was an unqualified success.» What does Sam mean? | It was shared with another school | *It was a complete success, with no limits | It was only for grown-ups | It was a failure
exam: Which phrase uses unqualified to mean complete? | *an unqualified success | unqualified electrician | unqualified to teach | unqualified doctor
